# Line endings are normalised to LF in the index AND in the working tree.
#
# Why eol=lf and not the default autocrlf behaviour: `reference_manifest.json`
# pins byte hashes for the fixed sources (S1 product baseline, S2 workbook).
# With core.autocrlf=true the checked-out index.html carried CRLF and hashed
# ffc57d96… while the committed blob hashed bfbc2756… — the manifest value.
# Same content, 1736 extra CR bytes. Pinning eol=lf makes the working tree
# match the manifest byte for byte, so hash verification is reproducible on
# any machine and CI diffs stay stable.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Received sources are byte-pinned by reference_manifest.json: NEVER normalise them.
# `* text=auto eol=lf` above would rewrite line endings on checkout, and some of these
# files were hashed with CRLF (mission_usuario.txt is one), so normalising them silently
# breaks `npm run verify:sources`. -text means "store and check out exactly these bytes".
full-implementation-pack/references/** -text
legacy/baseline/** -text
legacy/baseline/index.html -linguist-detectable

# Binary sources: never touch.
*.xlsx binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ico binary

# package-lock.json stays a normal diffable text file on purpose: a lockfile diff is
# exactly what a reviewer needs in order to audit a dependency change.
